WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.310
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:09.070
In 2008 an inmate in Colorado, convicted of
armed robbery, was released from prison 90

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.490
years too early, due to a clerical error.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:17.450
He couldn't believe his luck and was determined
to get his life back on track.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:22.470
He got a job, got married, had kids, went
to church every week and started coaching

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.000
youth soccer.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.640
After six years of making the most of his
accidental freedom, authorities realised their

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.239
mistake and sent him back to prison.

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:38.280
In America prison inmates are accidentally
released too early, disturbingly often.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.820
Also in Colorado another inmate was mistakenly
released four years early, their story wasn't

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:45.829
as pleasant.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:51.910
Whilst out of prison he killed the state's
correction chief when he knocked on the inmate's

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:52.910
door.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:58.100
But if an inmate serves their entire sentence
or is perhaps let out early for good behaviour,

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.219
what are the chances of them re-offending.

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:04.690
Does the prison system actually work?

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:10.020
There are currently over nine million people
incarcerated in prisons worldwide.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.570
Half of that number is held in just three
countries, the US, China and Russia.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:21.840
The US has the most prisoners by far, with
over 2.3 million adults behind bars within

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.130
its borders.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:31.330
America has just 5% of the world's population
but over one quarter of its prison population.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.260
So does this mass incarceration work?

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:41.490
No, absolutely not, in fact it does quite
the opposite.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.130
Prison only creates more crime and more prisoners.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.369
It is a completely self-criminalising system.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:52.990
Imagine if I told you I had a fantastic new
plan to stop crime.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:57.130
We take all the people that have been naughty
and put them into a building where they can't

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.689
leave and all they can do to keep themselves
occupied is talk to all the other criminals

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:03.760
and learn.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.479
Learn how to commit more complex crimes and
get away with it.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:13.529
And plan their next grand criminal escapade
once they are released.

00:02:13.529 --> 00:02:16.480
They may even form coalitions.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.230
Crime is practiced on one-another inside prison,
in whatever ways possible.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.029
Some rise to the top of the food chain and
become kingpins or gang leaders that command

00:02:26.029 --> 00:02:31.209
and orchestrate new crimes on the outside,
from inside prison.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:37.760
Many experts have labelled prison as "crime
school" and it's easy to see why.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.989
This is an excerpt from a letter by a UK prisoner
called Ben, that was entered into a yearly

00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:44.909
prisoner writing competition.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:48.919
"The day I was sent to prison, I learned how
to steal cars.

00:02:48.919 --> 00:02:54.409
Still in shock from the jury’s verdict,
I was led from the courthouse into a white

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:55.409
van.

00:02:55.409 --> 00:03:00.549
A convict in another compartment was boasting
of how he drove off and sold luxury motors:

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:02.959
BMWs, Mercedes and the like.

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:07.669
He used an electronic device to override their
immobilisers.

00:03:07.669 --> 00:03:13.040
Unseen figures from other compartments called
out questions: “How long does it take?”

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.670
“What models does it work on?”

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.569
“How can I get one?”

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:18.819
I listened, intrigued.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:22.439
I’d never stolen anything and didn’t intend
to.

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:26.659
Then again it sounded so easy …"

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:31.249
So then it's no surprise that the recidivism
rate is so high.

00:03:31.249 --> 00:03:39.290
In the US almost 70% of prisoners are rearrested
within three years of being released and 77%

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.889
are rearrested within five years.

00:03:41.889 --> 00:03:45.439
And that's just the people that are caught
recommitting crimes.

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:50.709
Sure, when it comes to any kind of prison
statistics, America is always the worst example,

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:57.879
but other countries are not far behind, in
the UK 47% of prisoners reoffend within three

00:03:57.879 --> 00:03:59.099
years.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:05.729
But there is one country that has broke the
mould and has reduced their recidivism rate

00:04:05.729 --> 00:04:06.969
to just 20%.

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:11.089
But we'll talk about that in a minute.

00:04:11.089 --> 00:04:15.209
Hatred breeds hatred and violence causes violence.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:19.949
People who have been hurt are the most likely
to go on to hurt others.

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:25.180
And as long as the prison system is based
on violence and hard-line punishment it will

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.010
continue to create more violence and crime.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.890
The world's leading psychologists have long
agreed that confining humans in cages with

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:40.680
minimal human interaction and sometimes with
no human interaction whatsoever, is unimaginably

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.300
damaging to one's long-term mental health.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.569
It creates deep mental wounds.

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.849
It de-humanises people and devalues a person's
sense of worth.

00:04:50.849 --> 00:04:56.470
And once you take that away, more crime can
seem like the only way forward after release

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:57.470
from prison.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:03.840
But by far one of the most damaging effects
of prison on both prisoners and society is

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.500
isolation, especially from family members.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:12.870
Prison tears away a person's support network
and research shows that the number one factor

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:18.360
that determines if a person will re-offend
or not after release, is whether they have

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.150
support from their family and friends.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.770
Prison makes this level of support practically
unachievable.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:34.449
But the wounds go even deeper, in America
2.7 million children have a parent inside

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:39.740
prison, research has shown that when a child
loses their mother or father figure to prison,

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:46.000
with minimal contact, they are many, many
times more likely to grow up to commit crime

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.500
themselves.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.330
So not only is prison causing adults to re-offend
its also preparing the next generation to

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.379
be criminals too.

00:05:55.379 --> 00:05:57.280
So what's the answer?

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.860
It's very well saying that prison doesn't
work but surely the only alternative, that

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.999
doesn't violate every human rights law, is
allowing murderers, rapists and other criminals

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.440
to roam the streets?

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.229
Or is it?

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:14.159
Remember earlier I mentioned that there is
a country where the reoffending rate is just

00:06:14.159 --> 00:06:15.159
20%.

00:06:15.159 --> 00:06:21.729
Well, what if I also told you that this mystical
place also has one of the lowest incarceration

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:23.500
rates in the world.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:30.110
They have put only 70 out of every 100,000
of their citizens behind bars.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:36.620
Compared to the world's leading incarcerator,
America, which has put 716 of every 100,000

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.419
of their citizens in prison, over 10 as many.

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:44.539
Welcome to a magical land called Norway.

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:50.150
Where the air is clean, the food is pickled
and the streets are ridiculously safe.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:56.699
Norway is officially the 6th safest country
in the world according to The Legatum Prosperity

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:03.270
Index of 2016, joining the ranks of other
Scandinavian countries and places like Singapore

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.509
and Japan.

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:11.189
Sure Norway has had its upsets in recent years
but overall its still one of the safest places

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:12.189
to live.

00:07:12.189 --> 00:07:17.360
Yet how can this be when they incarcerate
such a tiny percentage of their population?

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.560
Surely the only way to keep the public safe
is to lock up all the criminals for life so

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.770
they can't commit any more crime.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:32.759
That makes sense in theory, but the most important
factor is what happens after a prisoner is

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:34.169
released.

00:07:34.169 --> 00:07:37.490
There isn't a constant supply of new criminals.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:43.789
It's estimated that only 15% of the population
of a country will ever commit a crime during

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:49.319
their lives and the vast majority of major
crimes that are worthy of prison time are

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:56.120
committed by an even smaller group of constant
reoffenders, in most countries at least.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.469
So then it makes sense that if you reform
that tiny pool of people that are continually

00:08:01.469 --> 00:08:07.659
committing crimes, over and over again, by
turning them into fully functioning, upstanding

00:08:07.659 --> 00:08:15.610
members of society then the prison population
will naturally decrease instead of increasing

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.409
every year, as it has in America.

00:08:18.409 --> 00:08:25.249
And that's how Norway has created one of the
safest countries in the world and achieved

00:08:25.249 --> 00:08:31.279
one of the lowest prison populations, by mastering
the art of rehabilitation.

00:08:31.279 --> 00:08:36.370
In fact Norway's rehabilitation methods have
been so successful that their prison population

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.810
is falling every year and so is crime.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:43.190
Whilst the rest of the world is busy building
more and more prisons.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:47.260
But their method of rehabilitation is quite
unusual.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:51.959
Unlike in most developed countries, the prison
authorities don't sit you in a white room

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:56.320
and have a patronising conversation with you,
about how you will manage on the outside.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.269
They don't make you absolutely promise that
you'll get a job and not shoot anyone.

00:09:01.269 --> 00:09:08.380
What they do is actually refreshingly simple
and obvious, they treat you like a normal

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:09.880
person.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.350
This may seem unfair, after all these are
criminals, not puppies.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:19.470
But when you think about it, why shouldn't
criminals be treated like normal people, and

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:23.389
given proper accommodation, jobs and social
lives.

00:09:23.389 --> 00:09:29.660
They may have messed up, but your basic human
needs don't suddenly change when you get sentenced.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:34.820
And to strip away the essential things that
make you human only serves to cause further

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.310
mental damage to a person, increasing the
likelihood of them committing violent crime

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.030
in the future.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:48.149
Welcome to Halden, a maximum security prison
in Norway, that houses the countries most

00:09:48.149 --> 00:09:49.860
violent criminals.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:53.320
All inmates are treated the same, no matter
their crime.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.200
There is no pointless and damaging solitary
confinement.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.120
This is an inmate's cell, it's nice and roomy,
they all have unrestricted windows to the

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:11.800
outside with plenty of natural light, and
it's all theirs, they don't have to share

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:12.800
with anyone.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:18.089
There's a flatscreen TV in every room, private
shower, mini-fridge and other luxuries.

00:10:18.089 --> 00:10:21.270
I've definitely stayed in worse hotel rooms.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.750
There's a cookery school where all inmates
are taught how to cook for themselves.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:30.710
A shop and kitchen, so inmates can buy ingredients
and make their own meals or cook for their

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:35.600
prison buddies, and yes, there are plenty
of sharp knives lying around.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.089
Prisoners are free to leave their cells at
any time and enjoy the prisons various facilities,

00:10:40.089 --> 00:10:47.300
such as the gym, sports hall with a rock climbing
wall, outdoor sports field, music studio where

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:52.089
inmates are encouraged to make music that
is often played by local radio stations, a

00:10:52.089 --> 00:10:57.190
woodworking studio, a library with CDs, DVDs
and games.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:02.120
Not only are prisoners encouraged to spend
time outside their cells but they are actually

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.529
paid to do so.

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:10.579
Prisoners are given an incentive of 53 Kroner,
or $7 dollars per day to leave their cells

00:11:10.579 --> 00:11:12.190
and socialise.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:18.170
All inmates are offered the opportunity of
education and paid jobs within the prison.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:25.290
Nobody has ever tried to escape from Halden
prison and incidents of violence are extremely

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:26.550
rare.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:32.810
Norway is a vast, beautiful country and in
Norway taking away a person's freedom is considered

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:36.780
enough of a punishment for any crime.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:42.410
So instead of spending a person's prison sentence
punishing them further, Norwegian prisons

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:50.470
focus solely on rehabilitation and repairing
the mental damage that may have caused them

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:53.930
to commit crime in the first place.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:58.810
This may be too progressive for some and it
raises an ethical concern.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:05.670
Is it right and just that a person who potentially
murdered a friend or family member should

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:11.440
be treated as though they're at a five-star
retreat, at the cost of taxpayers?

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:18.950
But when viewed pragmatically it makes perfect
sense, this prison has achieved an incredibly

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:25.040
low recidivism rate of just 20%, the lowest
in the world.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:33.040
And this in turn, lowers crime rates every
year, it's better for society and the economy.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:38.380
This is all helped along further by the incredible
support a Norwegian ex-prisoner receives after

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.070
being released.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:44.569
Various agencies give them support and check
up on them regularly, making sure they have

00:12:44.569 --> 00:12:49.380
accommodation, a support network and crucially
a job.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:54.730
Contrary to America where a criminal record,
even for a minor offence is a big black mark

00:12:54.730 --> 00:13:00.380
on your CV and permanent government records,
which ensures you will never get a proper

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:05.259
job and can never develop a career for the
rest of your life.

00:13:05.259 --> 00:13:10.670
It goes without saying that if you take away
a criminal's chance to get a job, they're

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:13.389
probably going to turn to crime to fund themselves.

00:13:13.389 --> 00:13:15.350
Well, I'll be dammed!

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:22.120
In Norway ex-prisoners are given free healthcare,
education and a mandated pension.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.680
Just like any other citizen.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:29.829
Whilst many around the world have criticised
Norway for being too soft on crime, the numbers

00:13:29.829 --> 00:13:34.430
speak for themselves, with a 20% recidivism
rate.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:40.220
Punishment just doesn't work and it doesn't
provide a deterrent, punishment only exacerbates

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:45.500
the problem, many countries have made a compelling
case to prove that.

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:51.110
Norway has proven that the only way to solve
the crime issue is to work with criminals

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:54.090
to achieve a better future.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:58.680
And Norway have taken this one huge step further.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.310
Halden Prison may seem like a paradise to
many.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:05.569
I mean, most students across the world probably
live in worse conditions.

00:14:05.569 --> 00:14:12.060
But there's a prison that's even more luxurious,
a place that would give your last holiday

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.580
abroad a run for its money.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:17.500
Welcome to Bastoy Island in Norway.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:23.430
A beautiful secluded island, lush with forests
and fauna, with some of the most idyllic beaches

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:27.019
in the world, just 50 miles south of Oslo.

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:32.589
But this isn't a five star holiday resort,
this is a prison.

00:14:32.589 --> 00:14:38.370
Bastoy island is Norway's minimum-security
prison and the most luxurious prison in the

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:39.370
World.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.690
But this isn't where the small-time crooks
are held.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:47.220
Bastoy is home to murderers, rapists and child
molesters.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:53.149
There are no locks, chains, cells or razor
wire.

00:14:53.149 --> 00:14:57.930
Prisoners are free to roam the island at their
leisure, and enjoy bike rides, or go to the

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:01.930
beach for a dip in the ocean, whenever they
feel like it.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:06.889
Prisoners live in shared apartments and everyone
has their own private bedroom with a lock

00:15:06.889 --> 00:15:08.230
and key.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.500
Even the prison guards don't have a key to
prisoner's rooms.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:17.810
The wooden houses are flush with TVs, computers,
musical instruments and other luxuries.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:24.279
Island inmates can take free classes in music,
cookery, computer studies, craftsmanship or

00:15:24.279 --> 00:15:27.459
academia, to prepare them for work after their
release.

00:15:27.459 --> 00:15:32.089
They can apply for jobs on the island; most
work on the farm, looking after the animals,

00:15:32.089 --> 00:15:37.260
using heavy machinery and growing food to
feed the other islanders.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:42.490
They live within their own ecosystem and the
island is fully self-sufficient and sustainable.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:48.980
The island hosts around 120 inmates with only
five prison guards keeping watch overnight.

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:54.010
Even during the day, the inmates vastly outnumber
the prison staff, who say they have good friendships

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:59.699
with all the inmates and are regularly invited
in their homes for coffee and a chat when

00:15:59.699 --> 00:16:01.230
passing by.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:07.709
One of the previous residents of Bastoy Island
was sent here after he chopped up two people

00:16:07.709 --> 00:16:09.560
with a chainsaw.

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:16.519
Whilst on the island he was allowed to work
in the forest with a chainsaw.

00:16:16.519 --> 00:16:22.779
For recreation there is a sauna, tennis courts,
plenty of fishing spots and horseback riding.

00:16:22.779 --> 00:16:27.699
There is a resident head chef that cooks up
restaurant quality dishes for anyone who doesn't

00:16:27.699 --> 00:16:29.810
want to cook for themselves.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:34.439
Some longer-term residents have the freedom
to leave the island on boats and go to work

00:16:34.439 --> 00:16:37.480
at real jobs on the mainland, 5 days a week.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:42.580
No one has ever tried to escape from Bastoy
Island.

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:44.760
Well, why would you?

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:50.720
This is essentially a holiday camp for criminals
that many non-criminals would pay good money

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.589
to stay at.

00:16:52.589 --> 00:16:57.980
This infuriates many people, why should we
coddle and pamper violent criminals?

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:03.399
Well, in the words of the head psychologist
at Bastoy prison and its ex-governor Arne

00:17:03.399 --> 00:17:09.040
Nilsen "If we have created a holiday camp
for criminals here, so what?

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:15.410
We should reduce the risk of reoffending,
because if we don't, what's the point of punishment,

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:20.030
except for leaning toward the primitive side
of humanity?"

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:28.370
And its difficult to argue with him when Bastoy
Island prison's re-offender rate is just 16%,

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:34.530
even lower than the already impressive Norwegian
national average of 20%.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:38.610
The maximum prison sentence in Norway is just
21 years.

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:44.540
Even the terrorist Anders Breivik who, in
2011, killed eight people with a van bomb

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:52.230
in Oslo then chillingly shot dead 69 people
at a summer camp, was only given a sentence

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.470
of 21 years.

00:17:54.470 --> 00:18:01.120
Upon entering the court room the judge shook
Breivik's hand and smiled at him, as is customary

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:02.430
in Norway.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:08.230
This and his short sentance was greeted with
mass skepticism and disgust by the world's

00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:09.680
media.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:15.260
He is the biggest challenge Norway's idyllic
prison system has ever faced, will it prove

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:19.900
as successful at reforming him as it has many
more before him?

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:21.340
Only time will tell.

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:27.360
Whether you agree with Norway's views on rehabilitation
or not, you have to hand it to them, they

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:33.540
are doing something right, they have accomplished
one of the safest societies in the world without

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.680
ever really punishing anyone.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:42.120
And when hard line punitive treatment in America
and other countries has only made problems

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:50.010
far worse and increased violence by both criminals
and the authorities, year on year, perhaps

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:57.120
it is time for the world to pay attention.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:12.950
Thanks for watching.

